> Each turn of the machine's main wheel stands for a year, and the gears give the Moon's pointer 254 turns in 19 years. That ratio is a very little too slow against the stars. The machine's picture of how the Moon moves was good to about a degree, so it is the gear ratio that fails: its slow running added up to more than a degree within nine years. Since 205 BC it has dropped the pointer so far behind the Moon, most of the way round the dial, that the pointer now stands ahead of it: on 23 November 2026, 105° ahead, nearly a third of a turn. That is most of one turn lost in nearly thirty thousand.
